---
title: "How to use Codex CLI from your phone"
description: Follow, steer, and approve Codex CLI sessions from a phone, on Linux or macOS. Compares Pontia, OpenAI's ChatGPT remote control and Codex Cloud, and a do-it-yourself SSH and tmux setup.
published: "2026-11-11"
---

Codex CLI runs on the machine that has your repositories, toolchains, and credentials. Long tasks often stop on an approval prompt while you are away from that terminal. This article compares the ways to reach a running Codex session from a phone and which one fits which setup.

Pontia is the tool we build.

## The short answer

| Your situation | Best fit |
| --- | --- |
| Codex runs on Linux or macOS, including a headless server, and you want a phone UI with approval buttons | Pontia |
| Codex runs on a Mac or Windows PC with the ChatGPT desktop app | ChatGPT mobile remote control |
| The task can run from a clean checkout on OpenAI's machines | Codex Cloud |
| You prefer to assemble and maintain the remote access yourself | SSH or Mosh, tmux, and a private network |

## Option 1: Pontia

Pontia is a free, open-source remote control for coding agents running on your own machine. You install it once, sign in, and open [app.pontia.dev](https://app.pontia.dev) on your phone. There is no VPN, port forwarding, or terminal app to set up.

Codex CLI includes an app-server daemon. The ChatGPT desktop app also uses it, to run Codex on SSH hosts. Pontia's local service, `pontiad`, connects to that daemon and shows each Codex thread as a chat. From a phone you can:

- start a Codex session in a project directory;
- follow messages, tool calls, and command output as they stream;
- answer approval requests for commands, file changes, and extra permissions with **Allow** or **Deny**;
- send or queue follow-up messages, interrupt a turn, and switch models with `/model`;
- reference project files with `@`.

Codex runs on your machine with its own credentials, sandbox, and approval policy. Sessions persist, so you can start a task at your desk and continue it from your phone. The browser and your machine set up an end-to-end encrypted session through Pontia's relay, which only forwards ciphertext. Your machine connects out, so it needs no open inbound port.

Pontia supports Linux (x86_64 and ARM64) and macOS 13.5 or later, including headless servers.

### Setup

With Node.js and npm installed, run these commands on the machine as the user who runs Codex:

```sh
codex login --device-auth   # device code flow, works over SSH
npx @pontia/cli init       # select Codex
```

`pontia init` does the rest:

- it configures the Codex daemon to start automatically, with a systemd user service and lingering on Linux, or a LaunchAgent on macOS;
- it signs you in with Google or GitHub through a URL and one-time code, which you can approve from your phone;
- it registers the machine with an official relay.

When it finishes, open [app.pontia.dev](https://app.pontia.dev), pick the machine, and start a session.

The optional Codex launcher also makes threads you start with `codex` in a terminal appear in the Dashboard, so you can continue them from the phone. See [Codex setup](/docs/codex). Advanced users can [run their own relay](/docs/self-hosted-edge) instead of the official one.

## Option 2: ChatGPT mobile remote control

OpenAI's own remote control lets the ChatGPT mobile app start chats on a connected computer, steer active work, approve commands, and review diffs and terminal output. Work runs on the host with its files, credentials, and sandbox settings.

The host must be a Mac or Windows PC running the ChatGPT desktop app. OpenAI's [Remote connections guide](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/remote-connections) states that mobile setup starts from that app, and that you can't set it up from the Codex CLI or IDE extension. Workspace admins may need to enable Remote Control. To reach a Linux server this way, the desktop app connects to the server over SSH, so the Mac or Windows host must stay awake as well.

Use this if you already work in the ChatGPT desktop app on Mac or Windows.

## Option 3: Codex Cloud

Codex Cloud runs tasks on OpenAI-managed machines, so they continue while your computer sleeps, and you can start and continue them from the mobile app. A cloud task uses only the files and services in its cloud environment. OpenAI's [environment docs](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/environments/modes) note that your local files, running processes, and VPN access are not transferred to it.

Use this for work that can run from a clean checkout. It does not fit tasks that depend on a local database, a private network, hardware, or credentials you keep on your own machine.

## Option 4: Build it yourself with SSH and tmux

You can also assemble remote access from standard tools: tmux keeps Codex running, a private network makes the machine reachable, and a phone SSH client connects to it. With a hook and ntfy, the phone is also notified when Codex is waiting.

### 1. Make the machine reachable with Tailscale

Install Tailscale on the host and sign in:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://tailscale.com/install.sh | sh
sudo tailscale up
tailscale ip -4      # note the 100.x.y.z address
```

Install the Tailscale app on the phone and sign in with the same account. The host is now reachable from the phone at that address, with no open port on the internet.

For an always-on host, disable [key expiry](https://tailscale.com/docs/features/access-control/key-expiry) for it in the Tailscale admin console. Keys expire after 180 days by default, and the host then drops off the network.

The phone can run only one VPN at a time, so the Tailscale app turns off any other VPN or proxy app. If you need one of those, [Tailscale vs Cloudflare Tunnel](/blog/tailscale-vs-cloudflare-tunnel-for-coding-agents) covers Cloudflare Tunnel, which doesn't use the phone's VPN slot, and the address and routing conflicts between VPNs.

### 2. Run Codex in tmux, and keep its output scrollable

Codex draws its TUI on the terminal's alternate screen by default. tmux does not keep alternate screen content in its history, so after you reattach, earlier output is gone from tmux scrollback. Turn it off in `~/.codex/config.toml`:

```toml
[tui]
alternate_screen = "never"
```

Then start Codex in a named session:

```sh
tmux new -s codex
codex
# detach with Ctrl-b d
```

From the phone, connect and reattach:

```sh
mosh agent@100.x.y.z tmux attach -t codex
```

Mosh keeps the session alive when the phone switches between Wi-Fi and mobile data, or sleeps. It needs `mosh` installed on the host and a phone client that supports it. Without Mosh, use `ssh -t agent@100.x.y.z tmux attach -t codex` and reconnect after each drop. To scroll, enter tmux copy mode with `Ctrl-b [` and leave it with `q`.

If tmux itself died, for example after a reboot, start a new session and continue the last conversation with `codex resume --last`.

### 3. Get notified when Codex needs approval

Codex's `notify` setting fires only when a turn ends. [Hooks](https://developers.openai.com/codex/hooks) also run when Codex is about to ask for approval. Subscribe to a long random [ntfy](https://ntfy.sh/) topic in the ntfy phone app, then save this as `~/.codex/hooks/ntfy.sh` and make it executable. It needs `jq` and `curl`.

```sh
#!/bin/sh
url=https://ntfy.sh/YOUR_TOPIC

event=$(cat)
field() { printf '%s' "$event" | jq -r "$1"; }
project=$(field '.cwd | split("/") | last')

case $(field .hook_event_name) in
  PermissionRequest)
    title="Codex needs approval: $project"
    body=$(field '.tool_input.description // .tool_input.command // .tool_name')
    ;;
  Stop)
    title="Codex finished: $project"
    body=$(field '(.last_assistant_message // "Turn complete")[0:300]')
    ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

curl -fsS --max-time 5 -o /dev/null -H "Title: $title" -d "$body" "$url" || true
```

Register it in `~/.codex/config.toml`:

```toml
[[hooks.PermissionRequest]]
[[hooks.PermissionRequest.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "~/.codex/hooks/ntfy.sh"
timeout = 10

[[hooks.Stop]]
[[hooks.Stop.hooks]]
type = "command"
command = "~/.codex/hooks/ntfy.sh"
timeout = 10
```

Open `/hooks` in Codex and trust the new hooks. Codex skips untrusted hooks, and any later edit to the script or the entry needs trusting again. The script prints nothing, so Codex still shows its normal approval prompt. Printing a decision from a `PermissionRequest` hook would approve or deny the request instead. Drop the `Stop` entry if a notification after every turn is too much.

[Phone notifications for Codex and pi](/blog/coding-agent-phone-notifications) explains the hook payload and how to keep commands and paths out of a third-party notification server.

### What you end up with

The notification tells you an approval is waiting. To answer it, you open the SSH app, reattach, and press `y` or `Esc` in Codex's TUI on a phone keyboard. You maintain each piece yourself: Tailscale on both devices, SSH keys on the phone, tmux, and the hook.

## Pick an approval policy that works on a phone

Whichever option you choose, a phone is a poor place to review a long command. Codex's approval policy decides how often you will be asked. OpenAI's [approvals and security guide](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-approvals-security) describes the `Auto` preset, `--sandbox workspace-write --ask-for-approval on-request`. With it, Codex edits files and runs commands inside the workspace without asking, and asks before writing outside it or using the network.

To make it the default instead of passing flags, set it in `~/.codex/config.toml`:

```toml
sandbox_mode = "workspace-write"
approval_policy = "on-request"

[sandbox_workspace_write]
# Directories outside the project that Codex may write without asking,
# for example a shared build cache.
writable_roots = ["/home/agent/.cache/pnpm"]
```

`writable_roots` is optional. Use it for paths that otherwise trigger a request on every build, so the requests that reach your phone stay rare.

That suits remote supervision. Routine edits proceed, and the requests that reach your phone are the ones worth reading. Running with approvals disabled removes the reason to watch from a phone at all, so keep that for sandboxed or disposable machines.

